105	/* ── Cascading rise ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
106	 *
107	 * The desktop counterpart of the mobile `.pt-bounce` tier (index.css). Because
108	 * the chrome persists across navigations, this cascade IS the page transition —
109	 * every screen's blocks rise in sequence as the slot swaps.
110	 *
111	 * Two keyframes, expo-out, no scale. It used to be a three-overshoot bounce on
112	 * cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1); since CSS applies the timing function to
113	 * every keyframe interval, that curve's 9.8% overshoot compounded with the
114	 * keyframes and the tail ran to ~1.2s. See docs/PLAN-2026-08-15-motion-toning.md.
115	 *
116	 * Usage: put `.dt-cascade` on a container to auto-index its direct children, or
117	 * `.dt-rise` on any element and set `--dt-i` (step) / `--dt-d` (extra delay).
118	 */
119	@keyframes desktopRiseIn {
120	  from {
121	    opacity: 0;
122	    transform: translateY(10px);
123	  }
124	  to {
125	    opacity: 1;
126	    transform: none;
127	  }
